  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard in Kibana, so I haven't seen the panels render. I also haven't checked whether the rule has run yet.\n\n**Data**\n- The data view has `timestamp`, `bytes`, `clientip`, `extension`, `response`, `geo.*`, `machine.os`, `referer`, `url` and `tags`, among others.\n- `response` is a text field, so I used its `.keyword` subfield for grouping and matching.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-9c85cfc9 Web logs overview\"** (id `a17643cb-bb84-4fe7-9f52-a126a3d8e08f`)\n- It has six panels:\n  - Total requests\n  - Average bytes\n  - Unique clients\n  - Requests over time\n  - Requests by response code\n  - Requests by extension\n\n**Rule: \"EFF-9c85cfc9 5xx spike\"** (id `5fb2de2e-03ee-47f2-bbc6-d149269b3138`)\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It checks every minute and fires when the count of documents matching `response.keyword` against `5[0-9][0-9]` is above 10 in a 5-minute window.\n- It has no actions attached, so when it fires it only creates an alert in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone.\n- The sample data's timestamps are shifted to the present only when the data is installed. If they've aged, the rule may not see any recent 5xx requests.",
